course: dictionary
lesson: 3998
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The context, meaning the words around the word, tells you which meaning the writer chose. Look for clues first, then test your meaning in the sentence.
explain: «Light we see is only one small part of a much wider family of rays, from radio waves to gamma rays.» The clues are «family», «wider» and «radio waves». Gamma must be a kind of ray, not a letter, because it sits in the same family as radio waves.
explain: «Alpha, beta and gamma» is a list. Gamma is the third item, after alpha and beta. Here the context shows it is a label for position in a list, not a ray at all.
fact: Gamma is the third letter of the Greek alphabet, after alpha and beta. It is written γ (small) and Γ (capital).
fact: «Gamma rays» are very powerful, invisible rays.
fact: Gamma rays are given off by radioactive materials and by explosions in space. They can harm living things.
fact: Gamma rays are at the high-energy end of the electromagnetic spectrum, which also includes radio waves and X-rays.
fact: Earth's atmosphere blocks gamma rays, so telescopes that detect them have to work in space.
exercise: In the list «alpha, beta and gamma», which position is gamma? | *Third | Fourth | Second | First
exercise: Which letter is the third letter of the Greek alphabet? | beta | delta | *gamma | alpha
exercise: «Gamma rays» are very powerful and invisible. What are they? | Letters written in Greek | Types of food | *Rays given off by radioactive materials and explosions in space | Pieces of a list
test: Why do gamma-ray telescopes have to work in space? | *Because Earth's atmosphere blocks those rays | Because space is warmer | Because gamma rays only come from the Moon | Because telescopes need stars to see
test: Which clues in «from radio waves to gamma rays» tell you gamma is a kind of ray? | The word «letter» and the word «list» | The word «telescope» only | *The words «family», «wider» and «radio waves» | The word «small» only
exam: Which meaning fits «gamma rays» in a science lesson? | A third place in a race | A letter of the Greek alphabet | A type of cheese | *Very powerful, invisible rays from radioactive materials
